course: everyday
lesson: 11
title: Eyes up: noticing your surroundings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Being aware means using your eyes and ears to know what is happening around you, so that you spot trouble early and have time to act.
explain: Your brain can pay close attention to only a few things at once; when your eyes are on a screen, you can miss a car, a step or a friend waving right in front of you.
explain: Scientists showed people a video of players passing a ball and asked them to count the passes; about half of them did not notice a person in a gorilla suit walking right through the middle.
explain: A good habit: when you walk, cross a road or enter a new place, look up, look around and notice what is normal — then anything that changes stands out.
explain: A boy in Chicago crossed the road while texting and never looked up; he stepped in front of a car, and only the driver's fast braking saved him from being badly hurt.
explain: A girl in Bristol walked along a platform watching a video and drifted to the edge; a station worker shouted and pulled her back just before the train came in.
fact: Attention is limited: the brain cannot focus fully on two demanding things at the same time.
fact: People looking at a phone while walking drift off line and notice less around them.
fact: Missing something in plain sight because your attention is elsewhere is called inattentional blindness.
fact: Loud music in headphones can hide the sound of a car, a bike bell or a shout.
fact: Side vision (peripheral vision) is good at spotting movement but poor at seeing detail.
fact: When you enter a building, noticing where the exits are helps you leave quickly in an emergency.
fact: Knowing what is normal in a place makes it easier to spot something unusual.
fact: Before crossing a road, stop, put away your phone, and look and listen in both directions.
fact: In the USA and Canada traffic drives on the right, so the nearest cars come from the left.
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand traffic drives on the left, so the nearest cars come from the right.
fact: Every year many walkers are hurt because they were looking at a phone instead of the road.
fact: When your eyes are on a screen, you notice much less of what is happening around you, even things straight ahead.
exercise: Why is it risky to text while crossing a road? | Your attention is on the screen, so you may miss a car | The light of the screen confuses the drivers and dazzles them at night | Drivers cannot see people who hold phones | Texting makes the traffic lights change faster
exercise: What does being aware of your surroundings mean? | Using your eyes and ears to notice what goes on | Staring at one thing as hard as you can | Keeping your eyes closed to listen better | Remembering every word that people say to you all day
exercise: In the gorilla experiment, why did many people miss the gorilla? | Their attention was busy counting the passes | The gorilla was too small to see on the screen | The gorilla appeared for only a split second | They were looking away from the screen
exercise: Which part of your vision is best at catching movement? | Your side vision | The very centre of your vision | Only your left eye | Your vision while you blink
exercise: A boy in Texas is about to cross a street. From which side does the nearest traffic come? | From his left | From his right | From in front of him | From both sides at once
exercise: A girl in Leeds is about to cross a street. From which side does the nearest traffic come? | From her right | From her left | From behind her | From both sides at once
exercise: Why is it smart to notice the exits when you go into a cinema? | So you can leave quickly in an emergency | Because the best seats are near the exits | Because you must leave by the door you came in | Because staff will ask you to point to them
exercise: What can loud music in headphones do when you walk near traffic? | Hide the sound of a car or a bike bell | Make cars drive more carefully around you | Help you hear car horns more clearly | Make your side vision work better
exercise: What is a good way to use headphones near roads? | Take them off, or keep the sound very low | Turn them up so traffic noise does not bother you | Wear only the one on the side facing the road | Wear them only when it is dark outside
exercise: Why does knowing what is normal in a place help you? | Something unusual then stands out quickly | It lets you stop paying attention for good | It makes the place safer all by itself | It means nothing unusual can ever happen
exercise: Which is the safest habit when walking along a busy street? | Put the phone away and look around you | Walk fast with your eyes on your phone | Walk right on the kerb to see the cars | Look only at the ground so you do not trip
exercise: What is inattentional blindness? | Missing something in plain sight as your mind is busy | Being unable to see anything at all in the dark | Losing your sight from looking at screens for too many hours | Seeing things that are not really there
exercise: You watch a video while walking to school. What are you most likely to miss? | Things around you, like a car pulling out | Nothing, because your side vision sees it all | Only things that are very far away | Only sounds, but never things you could see
exercise: What should you do just before crossing a road? | Stop, put the phone away, look and listen both ways | Run across as fast as you possibly can | Cross while checking messages to save time | Look only the way that the cars usually come from on that road
test: Mia is texting while she walks through a car park. Why should she stop? | A car may back out and she might not notice | Car parks have no signal for phones | Drivers get angry at people with phones and may shout at her | Texting there will drain her battery
test: Which is true about attention? | The brain can fully focus on only a few things at once | The brain notices everything that the eyes see around it | Looking at a screen makes your hearing sharper | Attention grows stronger the more things you do
test: A boy in Sydney steps off the kerb after looking only to his left. Which danger did he miss? | Cars coming from his right, in the nearest lane | Cars coming from his left, in the nearest lane | Bikes, which only ride on the footpath | None, because cars always stop for people
test: You walk into a shopping centre with your eyes always on a game. What might you not know if a fire alarm rang? | Where the nearest exit is | What the time is | Which shop sells shoes | How many floors and lifts the building has
test: Why might a cyclist's bell not help a person wearing loud headphones? | The music covers the sound of the bell | Bells are too high for people to hear | Bells only work in the daytime | Cyclists ring bells only at crossings
test: Your friend says, "Anyone would notice a gorilla walking past." Which is true? | Many people miss it when their attention is busy | Everyone always notices big surprising things, like a gorilla | Only people with poor eyesight miss it | Only very young children ever miss it
test: What is the best reason to look up from your phone on a train platform? | To notice trains, the edge and people near you | Because phones stop working near trains | Because the staff will take your phone away if they see it out | Because it makes the train arrive sooner
test: Which helps you spot something unusual in your street? | Knowing what it usually looks like | Never looking at the street at all | Walking through it only at night | Listening to loud music as you go
test: What is your side vision good at noticing? | That something is moving | The words on a sign | The exact colour of small things | The details of a friend's face
test: walking n texting, keep bumping into stuff lol | Your attention is on the phone, not around you | Phones make you dizzy | Your eyes get tired from walking and looking down at the ground | Pavements are uneven now
test: in the cinema, why's dad looking for the exits | So we can get out fast in an emergency | To find the toilets | To check the film times on the board outside | Because he is bored
test: crossing in toronto, which way first | Left, the nearest cars come from the left | Right, the nearest cars come from the right | Straight up the road | Only behind you
test: crossing in sydney, which way first | Right, the nearest cars come from the right | Left, the nearest cars come from the left | Only straight ahead | It does not matter there
test: walking home and texting, road's quiet tho | Stop and put the phone away before you cross | Text faster so you finish before the road | Keep texting, quiet roads are safe | Hold the phone higher to see over it
test: almost walked into a lamp post lol watching tiktok | Look up when you walk; next time it could be a car | Walk slower and keep watching | Turn the sound up so you can hear what is around you | Only watch on short walks
test: Zoe says she can text and look at the road same time. true? | No, your attention can only be in one place | Yes, girls can do two things at once | Yes, if the text is short | Yes, if she uses one hand and holds the phone up high
test: they're saying a girl walked into a lamp post texting and needed stitches, it was on the news apparently | Maybe or maybe not on the news, but the lesson is real: put the phone away when you walk | It proves lamp posts are placed in silly spots | It only matters if you are texting, not watching videos | It is just gossip, so phones never stop you noticing
test: my mate reckons ur eyes can see everything around u even when ur on ur phone, it's like a superpower | That is wrong; side vision spots movement but not detail, and on a screen you notice much less | He is right, side vision sees every detail clearly | He is right, but only for teenagers | He is right, so you can cross roads while scrolling
test: Sophie walked into the glass door at the mall staring at her phone lol everyone's talking | Do not spread it to laugh at her; if anything, learn to keep your own eyes up | Spread it, because it is true and very funny | Spread it, so she gets known as clumsy | Spread it, but say it happened twice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phenomenon termed inattentional blindness? | The failure to perceive something in plain view because attention is directed elsewhere | The total loss of sight occasioned by excessive use of screens | The inability to perceive colours in conditions of dim light | The deliberate closing of the eyes when a person is frightened
exam: It being given that a pedestrian in the United States is about to cross a road, identify the direction from which the nearest traffic approaches. | From the left, traffic proceeding upon the right-hand side | From the right, traffic proceeding upon the left-hand side | From directly behind the pedestrian | From both directions with equal proximity
exam: Identify the principal benefit of noting the exits upon entering a building. | It permits a rapid departure in the event of an emergency | It permits the visitor to avoid paying for admission | It renders fire alarms within the building unnecessary | It permits the visitor to remain unseen by staff
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning peripheral vision. | It is proficient in the detection of movement but deficient in the perception of detail | It is proficient in the perception of detail but unable to detect movement | It is equal in every respect to central vision | It functions solely in conditions of darkness
